--TEST--
`Core\Script::args()` keeps the two absences apart: a child spawned with `args: []` reads an array of zero, and one spawned with no option at all reads `null` — `rule:core-classes/script-args`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# ADR 0118 § 2: `spawn script` goes through a door that asks for
# `script.spawn` first, and deny-by-default means a case with no
# configuration cannot spawn at all. This grants it and nothing else.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE shape.nvs--
<?nvs
// One reader for all three spawns below, so the three lines differ only in
// what the parent wrote. `mixed` is the type, so the answer has to be asked
// about rather than assumed: `null` is the absence, and anything else is the
// value in the shape it crossed in.
var $a = Core\Script::args();
if ($a == null) {
    echo "null\n";
} else {
    var $list = $a as array<string>;
    echo "array of ", Core\Arr::count($list), "\n";
}
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The distinction this case exists for: `args: []` is a value and no `args:`
// is not one. An empty array for both would collapse them, and a parent that
// writes `args: []` is saying something a parent that writes nothing did not.
var $two = spawn script "shape.nvs" with(args: ["alpha", "beta"], output: "capture");
var $none = spawn script "shape.nvs" with(args: [], output: "capture");
var $absent = spawn script "shape.nvs" with(output: "capture");

echo "two: ", (await $two)->output;
echo "empty: ", (await $none)->output;
echo "absent: ", (await $absent)->output;

// The answer is an ordinary value, so it is a legal thing to hand on: this
// root's own `null` crosses at `args:` and comes back as the absence it was.
// A member answering something the option could not carry would fail here
// rather than at the read.
var $relay = spawn script "shape.nvs" with(args: Core\Script::args(), output: "capture");
echo "relayed: ", (await $relay)->output;
--EXPECT--
two: array of 2
empty: array of 0
absent: null
relayed: null
